-- title:
--   Constructible sets stable under specialisation to closed points are closed
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a topological space (in a fixed universe) which is Noetherian, quasi-sober (every irreducible closed subset has a generic point) and Jacobson (the closed points are dense in every closed subset). Let $C \subseteq X$ be a constructible subset, i.e. $C$ lies in the Boolean algebra generated by the retrocompact open subsets of $X$. Assume that $C$ is stable under specialisation to closed points: for every $x \in C$ and every $y \in X$ with $x \rightsquigarrow y$ (that is, $y$ lies in the closure of $\{x\}$) such that the singleton $\{y\}$ is closed, one has $y \in C$. Then $C$ is closed in $X$. Note that the specialisation hypothesis is imposed only for closed points $y$, not for arbitrary specialisations; the conclusion nevertheless gives closedness, hence stability under all specialisations.
--
--   This is the standard statement that on a Noetherian quasi-sober Jacobson space closedness of a constructible set may be tested on closed points alone (as in EGA IV 10.4 and the Stacks Project). It is used in the Noetherian valuative criterion for universal closedness, [`AlgebraicGeometry.universallyClosed_of_forall_isDiscreteValuationRing_finite_residueField_hasLift`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.universallyClosed_of_forall_isDiscreteValuationRing_finite_residueField_hasLift).
